1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:03,650
Date fire scars and assess the nature of the injury.

2
00:00:04,105 --> 00:00:09,020
Giant-sequoia studies combine scars and ages to study fire history.

3
00:00:09,474 --> 00:00:13,928
A fire may leave no scar: no scar does not prove no fire.

4
00:00:14,382 --> 00:00:17,711
Date the injury and verify its likely cause.

5
00:00:18,171 --> 00:00:23,232
The absence of a scar alone does not demonstrate that no fire occurred.

6
00:00:24,035 --> 00:00:27,411
Show recording periods alongside scar dates.

7
00:00:27,866 --> 00:00:31,353
Separate not recording from recording without a scar.

8
00:00:31,814 --> 00:00:35,360
This is a fictional example for the next exercise.

9
00:00:35,814 --> 00:00:41,511
In this synthetic example, lines show recording intervals and points show scars.

10
00:00:41,971 --> 00:00:45,580
The number of usable trees changes through time.

11
00:00:46,383 --> 00:00:49,818
Count recording trees and scarred trees for each year.

12
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:55,425
Percentage = scarred trees / recording trees times 100%.

13
00:00:55,885 --> 00:01:00,131
Does the table alone establish fire size or periodicity?.

14
00:01:00,438 --> 00:01:03,701
Pause to count recording and scarred trees by year.

15
00:01:04,162 --> 00:01:09,992
Do not treat non-recording intervals as zero-scar observations in the denominator.

16
00:01:10,795 --> 00:01:16,447
Three scarred trees mean 3/3 in 1940 but 3/4 in 1960.

17
00:01:16,903 --> 00:01:21,942
Recorded dates are 20 years apart; this does not prove periodic fire.

18
00:01:22,403 --> 00:01:26,804
These sample percentages are not burned area or fire severity.

19
00:01:27,258 --> 00:01:32,422
The same three scarred trees yield different percentages when the denominator changes.

20
00:01:32,876 --> 00:01:35,964
This is not a burned-area percentage.
